among logicians, a <a class="xref" href="vol20.html#article-TERM">term</a> denoting the first indirect <a class="xref" href="vol14.html#article-MODE">mode</a> of the first <a class="xref" href="vol8.html#article-FIGURE">figure</a> of syllogism. A syllogism in baralipiton, is when the two first <a class="xref" href="vol0.html#article-PROPOSITIONS">propositions</a> are general, and the third particular, the middle term being the subject in the first proposition and the predicate in the <a class="xref" href="vol19.html#article-SECOND">second</a>. The following is of this kind.

BA. Every evil ought to be feared;
RA. Every violent <a class="xref" href="vol16.html#article-PASSION">passion</a> is an evil;
LIP. Therefore something that ought to be feared is a violent passion.

among logicians, the first <a class="xref" href="vol14.html#article-MODE">mode</a> of the first <a class="xref" href="vol8.html#article-FIGURE">figure</a> of syllogisms. A syllogism in barbara is one whereof all the <a class="xref" href="vol0.html#article-PROPOSITIONS">propositions</a> are <a class="xref" href="vol20.html#article-UNIVERSAL">universal</a> and affirmative; the middle <a class="xref" href="vol20.html#article-TERM">term</a> being the subject of the first proposition, and <a class="xref" href="vol0.html#article-ATTRIBUTE">attribute</a> in the <a class="xref" href="vol19.html#article-SECOND">second</a>.

*Example.* BA. Every wicked <a class="xref" href="vol0.html#article-MAN">man</a> is miserable;
BA. All tyrants are wicked men;
RA. Therefore all tyrants are miserable.
